Callbacks
What is a Callback?
A callback is a function that is passed as an argument to another function and is executed later when a specific task is completed.
A callback is a function that "calls back" after another function finishes its work.
Syntax
function greet(name, callback) {
    console.log("Hello " + name);
    callback();
}

function bye() {
    console.log("Good Bye!");
}

greet("Ekta", bye);
Output
Hello Ekta
Good Bye!
Callbacks are mainly used when:
A task takes time to complete.
We want another function to execute only after the first task finishes.
Handling asynchronous operations like:
API calls
Reading files
Timers
Database operations
Example 1: Simple Callback
function display(message) {
    console.log(message);
}

function calculate(a, b, callback) {
    let sum = a + b;
    callback(sum);
}

calculate(10, 20, display);
Output
30
Example 2: Callback with setTimeout()
function message() {
    console.log("This runs after 3 seconds");
}

setTimeout(message, 3000);

console.log("Program Started");
Output
Program Started
(This waits 3 seconds)
This runs after 3 seconds
Advantages of Callbacks
Easy to understand for small programs
Useful for asynchronous tasks
Allows execution after completion of another task
Disadvantages of Callbacks
Difficult to manage when many callbacks are nested.
Leads to Callback Hell.
Hard to debug.
Reduces code readability.
Callback Hell
When callbacks are nested inside callbacks multiple times, the code becomes difficult to read and maintain.
This situation is called Callback Hell or Pyramid of Doom.
Example
login(function() {

    getUser(function() {

        getOrders(function() {

            getPayment(function() {

                console.log("Completed");

            });

        });

    });

});
Notice how the code keeps moving towards the right.
What is Synchronous JavaScript?
JavaScript executes code line by line.
It waits for one statement to finish before moving to the next.
This is called Synchronous Execution.
Example
console.log("A");

console.log("B");

console.log("C");
Output
A
B
C
Everything executes in sequence.
Problem with Synchronous Programming
Suppose one task takes 10 seconds.
console.log("Start");

waitFor10Seconds();

console.log("End");
Output
Start
(wait 10 seconds)
End
During those 10 seconds, nothing else happens.
This makes applications slow.
Asynchronous JavaScript -
Asynchronous JavaScript allows long-running tasks to execute without blocking the rest of the program.
Instead of waiting, JavaScript continues executing the remaining code.
Example
console.log("Start");

setTimeout(function() {
    console.log("Hello");
}, 3000);

console.log("End");
Output
Start
End
(after 3 seconds)
Hello
JavaScript does not wait for setTimeout().


How JavaScript Handles Asynchronous Code
JavaScript uses:
Call Stack - The Call Stack is the place where JavaScript keeps the function that is currently executing. Imagine JavaScript as a teacher who can do only one task at a time. It keeps a list of tasks to perform. That list is called the Call Stack. 
Web APIs - Browser features that handle asynchronous tasks like timers
Callback Queue (Task Queue) - The Callback Queue is a waiting area. When an asynchronous task (like setTimeout) finishes, its callback function waits here until JavaScript is free.
Event Loop - A manager that checks whether the Call Stack is empty. If it is, it moves the next callback from the Callback Queue to the Call Stack.
Example
console.log("Start");

setTimeout(function() {
    console.log("Hello");
}, 2000);

console.log("End");
Step 1 - Call Stack
console.log("Start")
Output
Start
Step 2
setTimeout() goes to Web APIs.
The timer starts.
JavaScript does not wait.
Step 3
Next line executes.
console.log("End")
Output
End
Step 4
After 2 seconds,
The callback function moves to the Callback Queue.
Notice: The callback does not directly enter the Call Stack. It first waits in the Callback Queue.
Step 5
The Event Loop checks whether the Call Stack is empty.
If No → Wait.
If Yes → Take the first callback from the Callback Queue and push it into the Call Stack.
Here, after console.log("End"), the Call Stack is empty.
So the Event Loop moves the callback.

Step 6
The callback executes.
Output
Hello



Call Stack
The Call Stack stores the functions that JavaScript is currently executing. 
Callback
A function passed to another function that will run later. In this example: function() { console.log("Hello"); }
Web APIs
Browser features that handle asynchronous tasks like timers, network requests, etc.
Callback Queue
A waiting line where completed callback functions wait before they can execute.
Event Loop
A manager that checks whether the Call Stack is empty. If it is, it moves the next callback from the Callback Queue to the Call Stack.

